 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 532944,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Suppose that the plane takes off at a <span data-math=\"{23^ \\circ }\">{23^ \\circ }</span> angle, and 1 mile from the airport is a 1,500-foot peak. At what altitude will the plane pass over the peak?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>First, convert one mile to 5,280 feet.</p>\n<p>The sine of an angle is the opposite side divided by the hypotenuse: <span data-math=\"\\sin \\alpha = \\frac{{{\\text{opp}}}}{{{\\text{hyp}}}}\"></span>.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"\\sin 23^\\circ = \\frac{y}{{5,280}}\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"y = 5,280\\left( {\\sin 23^\\circ } \\right)\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"y{\\text{ }} \\approx {\\text{ }}2,241{\\text{}}\"></span> feet</p>\n<p>The plane is approximately 2,241 feet in the airport when it passes over the peak.</p>\n<p>Note: <span data-math=\"2,241{\\text{ }}-{\\text{ }}1,500{\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}741\"></span> feet, so the plane is 741 feet over the peak.</p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "2,241 ft"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
